  "transcribed_text": " A message leaves host A at a local reading of 100 milliseconds.  Host B records its arrival at 97.  Has the message arrived before it was sent?  In this illustrative example, a shared reference shows the arrival at 103 milliseconds.  B's clock is 6 milliseconds behind that reference.  Sorting the local timestamps alone reverses the apparent sequence.  The matching message identity connects the send to its receive.  That dependency establishes causal order.  Unrelated events don't acquire a causal relationship simply because one local clock shows an earlier time.  Timestamps remain useful when their offset and uncertainty are understood.  Now separate that ordering question from performance.  Consider one operation requiring four participants with no overlapping work.  In the first example, all four contributions are ready at 10 milliseconds.  In the second, three are ready at 5 milliseconds and one at 25.  Both sets average 10 milliseconds.  Yet the group is ready at 10 in the first example and 25 in the second.  The second group takes 15 milliseconds longer to become ready.  It's three early participants each wait 20 milliseconds, 60 participant milliseconds altogether.  That aggregate waiting time is different from elapsed delay.  These four samples demonstrate a dependency, not a diagnosis or a reliable percentile estimate.  Investigate the affected steps using participant readiness, network and compute evidence,  and correlated operation identities.",
